.TH CVEC_CONSTRUCT_MP_SCOPED 3 "2026-09-20" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Macros"
.SH NAME
cvec_construct_mp_scoped \- construct a vector with a custom allocator, destroyed automatically at scope exit
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <ccollections/cvector.h>

cvec_construct_mp_scoped(v, type, mprocs);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
Declares a vector variable named v that holds elements of the given type,
creates the vector through mprocs, and adds a cleanup handler that
destroys the vector when control leaves the surrounding scope. It is
cvec_construct_mp(3) plus that handler, with one more difference: the
companion type variables are initialized to NULL here, while
cvec_construct_mp(3) leaves them indeterminate on purpose. Everything
else cvec_construct_mp(3) documents holds here too, including the
private copy of the procedure table that the vector keeps and the stop of
the program through _ccol_fatal_err() on a failed creation.

The variable is set to NULL before the vector is created, which makes the
handler safe on a path that leaves the block between the declaration and
a successful creation. The vector is destroyed on every exit from the
scope: the closing brace, a return, a break, a continue or a goto out of
the block. The variable is set to NULL as the vector is destroyed, so an
explicit cvec_destroy(3) earlier in the block doesn't lead to a double
free.

The vector goes back through the same procedures that created it, so
those functions must be callable at the end of the scope.
.fi
.SH PARAMETERS
.nf
v       Name of the vector variable to declare and create
type    Element type, for example int, double or struct foo
mprocs  Pointer to a ccol_memmgmt_procs_t. Its malloc, calloc, realloc
        and free members must all be set. Pass NULL for the default
        allocator.
.fi
.SH RETURN VALUE
.nf
None. The macro expands to declarations followed by a statement, not to
an expression.
.fi
.SH NOTES
.nf
When the vector can't be created the macro stops the program through
_ccol_fatal_err(), with a message that names v and the underlying error.

The macro relies on the cleanup attribute and so needs GCC or Clang. On a
toolchain without that attribute, use cvec_construct_mp(3) with an
explicit cvec_destroy(3).

Since it expands to declarations and then a statement, the macro has to
go where declarations are legal and can't be the body of an if or a loop
without braces. v is pasted into the names of the companion variables, so
it must be a plain identifier.

If the vector holds pointers to heap buffers, the elements are not freed
one by one, so free the element data before leaving the scope.
.fi
.SH WARNINGS
.nf
The vector must not outlive its scope. Don't return v, and don't store it
anywhere that code reads after the end of the block. When ownership has
to leave the block, use cvec_construct_mp(3).
.fi
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
void process(ccol_memmgmt_procs_t *procs) {
    cvec_construct_mp_scoped(vec, int, procs);

    cvec_push(vec, 42);

    if (cvec_size(vec) != 1) {
        return;   /* vec is destroyed here */
    }
}                 /* and here */
.fi
